# Files excluded from the Cloudflare upload.
#
# Cloudflare reads the "assets directory" literally: it is the repository root,
# so anything left in here becomes a public asset by default. Each group below
# has to be excluded explicitly.

# --- Git metadata ---------------------------------------------------------
# This is what broke the build on 2026-09-17.
#
# Pages used to skip .git on its own; Workers Static Assets does not -- it
# uploads the whole .git directory verbatim. The packfile inside is 82.6 MiB,
# well past the 25 MiB per-asset limit, so the build died during packaging:
#   Asset too large. ... We found a file /opt/buildhome/repo/.git/objects/
#   pack/pack-9bb9c06177198b6167aa254cec0e70c25e7f3512.pack with a size of 82.6 MiB.
#
# Pages' exclusions were implicit, Workers' are not, so migrating between the
# two silently reintroduces this. Same line the migration guide recommends.
**/.git

# --- Legacy archive tree (absent from the current tree) -------------------
# `assets/photos/` used to hold the camera masters, 4-33 MB each -- one of them
# 31.8 MiB -- and it is still what .gitignore points at. Nothing uses it now:
# the published photos ARE the originals, and they live under assets/opt/, which
# photo-data.js references. The old tree stays retrievable from git history:
#   git log --diff-filter=D -- assets/photos | head
#
# Kept as a guard so restoring that tree cannot silently re-break a build.
#
# FOOTGUN: the deployed originals live in assets/opt/, NOT here. If the album
# tree is ever moved or renamed under assets/photos/, every photo would be
# excluded and the site would ship with no images. Re-check this rule before
# renaming anything under assets/.
assets/photos

# --- Build and dev leftovers ----------------------------------------------
# The publish directory is the repository root, so none of these belong online.
scripts
**/node_modules
**/.DS_Store

# Wrangler's own scratch directory. Running `wrangler deploy --dry-run` INSIDE
# this folder (which is the documented workflow) creates `.wrangler/tmp/...`,
# and the next real deploy would upload those as public assets. It is rebuilt
# on every run, so deleting it by hand is not a fix.
**/.wrangler
